@page "/how-it-works"

<PageTitle>How BlokeBot works</PageTitle>

<article class="article-shell article-shell--wide">
    <header class="article-header article-header--split">
        <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
            <h1>Channel tools with a clear boundary.</h1>
        </div>
        <ul>
<li>The server owner operates BlokeBot.</li>
<li>Channel owners select the tools for their channels.</li>
<li>Moderators act within their current Twitch authority.</li>
</ul>
    </header>

    <ol class="process-list process-list--timeline">
        <li>
            <span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Identify</p>
                <h2>Sign in to your normal Twitch account</h2>
                <p>BlokeBot finds your channel and the channels that you currently moderate. You select one before you view or change its tools.</p>
                <a class="text-link" href="guide/getting-started">Sign in and select a channel</a>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Authorize</p>
                <h2>Connect the channel and bot accounts separately</h2>
                <p>The channel account grants channel-level access. The bot account grants chat and announcement access. The dashboard identifies the account for each step.</p>
                <a class="text-link" href="connect">Review Twitch connections</a>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Configure</p>
                <h2>Enable only the applicable tools</h2>
                <p>Each Chat Tools feature has a separate switch and settings. Community progression and games are separate features. BlokeBot isolates the data for each channel.</p>
                <a class="text-link" href="tools">Select channel tools</a>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Operate</p>
                <h2>Use live controls and respond to visible failures</h2>
                <ol>
            <li>Start the bot.</li>
            <li>Run rounds and giveaways.</li>
            <li>Review Alerts for delivery or readiness problems.</li>
</ol>
                <p>BlokeBot checks moderator authority again during a login session.</p>
                <a class="text-link" href="troubleshooting">Review alerts and recover</a>
            </div>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <section class="explain-grid" aria-labelledby="ownership-heading">
        <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">Data locations</p>
            <h2 id="ownership-heading">Public dashboard and private state</h2>
            <p>BlokeBot stores its database and OAuth tokens on its server. Public feature pages show only their documented views for participants.</p>
            <p>Configuration requires an authorized Twitch identity.</p>
            <ul class="check-list">
                <li>Channel data remains in the configured data directory of the server.</li>
                <li>Environment files or a secret manager hold Twitch client secrets.</li>
                <li>BlokeBot or a reverse proxy terminates HTTPS before OAuth reaches the app.</li>
                <li>Each backup contains the complete private application state.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <DeviceShowcase
            LightLaptopSource="media/dashboard/laptop-light-channel-setup.png"
            DarkLaptopSource="media/dashboard/laptop-dark-channel-setup.png"
            LightPhoneSource="media/dashboard/phone-light-channel-setup.png"
            DarkPhoneSource="media/dashboard/phone-dark-channel-setup.png"
            PhoneAlt="Example BlokeBot channel setup on mobile shows Twitch readiness. Account-specific connection actions and bot controls are visible."
            LaptopAlt="Example BlokeBot channel setup on a laptop shows Twitch readiness. Account-specific connection actions and bot controls are visible."
            Caption="Channel setup shows authority and connection status. The next action appears in the same place." />
    </section>

    <aside class="next-panel next-panel--strong" aria-labelledby="how-next-heading">
        <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">Choose your next step</p>
            <h2 id="how-next-heading">Use a channel or run the server?</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="action-row">
            <a class="button button-primary" href="guide/getting-started"><SiteIcon Name="book" />Use BlokeBot</a>
            <a class="button button-secondary" href="server-owners"><SiteIcon Name="server" />Run BlokeBot</a>
        </div>
    </aside>
</article>
